<!-- BEGIN rules:lang/typescript -->
## Guidance On TypeScript

- Keep TypeScript strict. Avoid `any`.
- Use exhaustive switches with no `default` case over externally defined unions, so upstream changes surface as compile errors.
- Never leave a promise rejection unobserved. Never write an empty `catch` block.
- Use only the package manager the project names. Do not switch to another unless a human explicitly asks.
<!-- END rules:lang/typescript -->

## Project Guidance

- Documentation
    - After adding or changing a reference dependency (Hermes gateway methods/events, SDK version), update docs/refs.md.
- Programmatic checks
    - Run `pnpm run typecheck` and `pnpm run test` after a logical set of changes is complete. Run `pnpm run build` when transport/entry code changed, followed by a smoke run of `dist/index.js`.
    - The pre-commit hook lives at `.githooks/pre-commit` (installed via `core.hooksPath` by `pnpm install`). It runs typecheck, tests, build, and a smoke run of `dist/index.js`; binaries are compiled only by the release workflow. Stage relevant changes before running it.
- Error handling
    - A failed Hermes turn must surface as a protocol error or typed failure, never as a clean `end_turn` (this is one of the upstream defects this project exists to fix).
    - Permission-like round-trips (approvals, elicitations) fail closed: an unanswered or stale request resolves to denial, never to allow.
    - Error messages identify the failed layer: gateway transport, gateway method, ACP method, or mapping layer.
- TypeScript
    - tsc is typecheck-only; esbuild owns emit.
    - Never advertise an ACP capability the adapter does not actually implement.
    - Shared constants belong in src/constants.ts.
    - Keep `src/index.ts` thin: transport wiring and method registration only. ACP semantics, gateway semantics, and event mapping stay in their own layers (see docs/todos.md for the layering).
    - Keep event mappers pure (gateway event in, ACP update out); confine dedup/ordering state to the per-turn handler.
- Tests
    - Adapter tests default to the snapshot style: scripted gateway events in, recorded ACP transcript out, with no network and no real Hermes.
    - Refrain from claiming an ACP feature is supported before it passes against a real client end-to-end.
- Security
    - Credentials never travel over ACP; the gateway process inherits them from the Hermes environment.
    - Do not log prompt text, file contents, or secret values by default.
